@startuml
title Cross-account S3 access: Account A assumes IAM Role in Account B

autonumber

actor "User / Application\nAccount A" as User
participant "IAM Principal\nAccount A" as PrincipalA
participant "AWS STS" as STS
participant "IAM Role\nDataReader\nAccount B" as RoleB
participant "IAM Policy\nEvaluation Engine" as IAM
participant "S3 Bucket\nsecret-bucket\nAccount B" as S3

User -> PrincipalA: Use Account A credentials

PrincipalA -> STS: AssumeRole\nRoleArn = arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:role/DataReader\nExternalId = unique-id-12345

note right of PrincipalA
Account A identity-based policy

This policy is attached to the IAM user or role
in Account A.

It allows the Account A principal to call
sts:AssumeRole on the Account B role.

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:role/DataReader"
    }
  ]
}
end note

STS -> IAM: Evaluate caller permissions

note right of IAM
AWS checks whether the Account A principal
is allowed to call sts:AssumeRole.

Evaluation can include:

1. Identity-based policy
2. Permission boundary, if any
3. SCP, if AWS Organizations is used
4. Explicit deny checks

Explicit Deny always wins.
end note

IAM --> STS: sts:AssumeRole is allowed

STS -> RoleB: Check role trust policy

note right of RoleB
Account B role trust policy

This policy is attached to the IAM role
in Account B.

It defines WHO can assume the role.

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:root"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "sts:ExternalId": "unique-id-12345"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
end note

RoleB --> STS: Trust policy allows Account A principal

STS -> STS: Generate temporary security credentials

STS --> PrincipalA: Return temporary credentials\nAccessKeyId + SecretAccessKey + SessionToken

note over PrincipalA, STS
After AssumeRole succeeds, Account A receives
temporary credentials for the Account B role.

The effective identity becomes:

arn:aws:sts::ACCOUNT_B_ID:assumed-role/DataReader/session-name

The caller now accesses S3 using the temporary
credentials of the Account B role.
end note

PrincipalA -> S3: GetObject request\nSigned with temporary role credentials

S3 -> IAM: Authorize S3 request

note right of S3
Permission policy attached to Account B role

This defines WHAT the assumed role can do.

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::secret-bucket/*"
    }
  ]
}
end note

IAM -> IAM: Evaluate final access decision

note right of IAM
For S3 access, AWS evaluates:

1. Explicit Deny
2. SCPs
3. Permission boundary
4. Session policy, if passed during AssumeRole
5. Role permission policy
6. S3 bucket policy
7. KMS key policy, if object uses SSE-KMS

For AWS Professional exam:

S3 permission alone is not enough if the object
is encrypted with a customer-managed KMS key.
The role also needs kms:Decrypt permission,
AND the KMS key policy must permit the role.
end note

alt Access allowed
    IAM --> S3: Allow
    S3 --> PrincipalA: Return object
else Access denied
    IAM --> S3: Deny
    S3 --> PrincipalA: AccessDenied
end

@enduml
